import{_ as t,o as n,c as i,a3 as r}from"./chunks/framework.BwzYn71E.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"jnk’s Hypotheses about the Underlying Logic of Several Issues","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/jnks-hypotheses-about-the-underlying-logic-of-several-issues"}]],"lang":"en-US","translationSource":{"title":"jnk 对一些问题的底层逻辑推测","archivedAt":"2026-10-23T14:20:34.857Z","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=4uninRFaljKfQH0mxWe1B9","scope":"page"}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"jnks-hypotheses-about-the-underlying-logic-of-several-issues.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-4uninRFaljKfQH0mxWe1B9.md","lastUpdated":null}'),s={name:"jnks-hypotheses-about-the-underlying-logic-of-several-issues.md"};function o(a,e,l,c,p,h){return n(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<div class="logistics-content"><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="jnk-对一些问题的底层逻辑推测" aria-hidden="true"></span><h1 id="jnk-s-hypotheses-about-the-underlying-logic-of-several-issues" tabindex="-1">jnk’s Hypotheses about the Underlying Logic of Several Issues <a class="header-anchor" href="#jnk-s-hypotheses-about-the-underlying-logic-of-several-issues" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;jnk’s Hypotheses about the Underlying Logic of Several Issues&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>4uninRFaljKfQH0mxWe1B9</code> | <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=4uninRFaljKfQH0mxWe1B9" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p>BV12zSfBeEkP<br> Normally, when a factory output is flush against a converger, more logistics components mean lower priority. But calculating terminal order—a certain ordering of logistics components connected to downstream factory inputs, treating the final component as a factory if none is downstream—is impossible with only one component. That case has special rules depending on the connection order between the component and upstream factory, and between that factory and its input. Moving/replacing the component and factory input to change their order visibly changes output priority.</p><p>BV1yVDjBLEYe<br> For the splitters and convergers in this structure, the first pair can be ignored: feed two full belts separately into the second converger and splitter to observe similar behavior. Their damping / level, roughly like downstream component count, can match or differ. When the terminal belt on splitter–belt–factory is connected first (an imprecise description of terminal order), splitter damping is computed through that route, equaling the converger&#39;s damping; the earlier-placed one is computed first. Otherwise damping is computed through the other route upstream of the converger, giving higher damping and later computation.<br> With equal damping, a splitter seeing a converger and belt downstream checks whether the converger&#39;s output was connected first. If so, it tries to make the converger output before itself, freeing space for normal splitting. When the converger computes first, the splitter has lower damping than the converger&#39;s other input and computes first, gaining priority and splitting normally.<br> With unequal damping, normal merge priority applies: the upstream component placed first has priority.<br> Different outcomes can be obtained by replacing the terminal factory and changing placement order of components flush against the converger&#39;s upstream and downstream sides.</p><p>BV1TJQKBxEph<br> This uses the preceding video&#39;s principle. Output depends on connection order between splitter and converger and between converger and downstream. Changing placement order of the downstream belt and upstream splitter changes it. Here is what occurs between splitter and converger in the two cases.<br> Belt placed later: the splitter does not first try to make the converger output. An item remains in the converger, so the splitter cannot output to it; one recirculating item stalls two splitter items.<br> Belt placed earlier: splitter output makes the converger output first, allowing normal splitting.</p><p>BV1KxdGBJETp<br> Yes, it involves timing. The two cases have different terminal orders. Among components ordered for the same terminal factory, non-splitters merge into the foremost entry, also the starting point of downstream-to-upstream BFS. This produces different damping values, which are not overwritten. In the left case, the right Protocol Stash was presumably placed first, so the splitter, upstream of the right-hand belt, computes after it. The belt has already output when the splitter does, allowing normal splitting. In the right case, there is only one terminal; all belts simultaneously start BFS. The splitter receives lower damping than the right-hand belt and tries output first, while that belt still holds an item and blocks it.</p><p>BV1FJ546LEg1<br> I cannot be certain, but this resembles a “4n, 1s stall”: when upstream computes first and downstream is full, upstream output lags by one minimum-distance unit. The first splitter produces a fluid splitting pattern of 0111. When the first two of three consecutive units fill the next pipe, the final unit and those following are blocked by one minimum distance. This resembles your latest video BV1nG346uEVg. A belt&#39;s minimum distance is 1/8 tile, while its minimum displayed distance is 1/2 tile. With a one-tile downstream belt, the second item after filling it stalls by 1/8; only after four stalls, four items total, does the 1/2-tile minimum visible distance appear. To test this, add a priority merge at the output. Since each item lags another 1/8 tile, even priority output permits a secondary input to enter. With a multi-tile belt, one secondary item naturally merges after a larger number of items.<br> BV1heGW6nE18<br> This is exactly the verification described in the first paragraph.</p></div>',1)])])}const m=t(s,[["render",o]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
